Title: Nick and Me

Credit: Written by

Author: Author

Source: Adapted from book

Draft date: 7/31/2026

Notes: Period live-action drama.

FADE IN:

INT. LOWELL'S RESTAURANT - DAY

NARRATOR, now older and broad-shouldered, sits alone beside the windows overlooking Seattle's gray water. A cinnamon roll cools on his plate.

(SOUND: ferry horn, coffee cups, rain ticking against the glass)

NARRATOR

Years of change, years of loneliness, and years of hatred for my brother. To understand why, I have to go back to Milwaukee.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

A big brick fifth-floor walk-up. NARRATOR, a lean young man with plain brown hair, struggles up the stairs behind NICK, already huge, scarred at the chin and left eyebrow, with reddish-brown hair and a rough, pockmarked face. NICK carries six grocery bags.

(SOUND: grocery bags creak, heavy boots pound the stairs)

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. I lived with him and Ma after Dad took off when I was about eight.

MA, an older woman with failing sight and brown hair gone gray, waits at the apartment door.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Nick became my father, my protector, and the leader of everything.

EXT. KIRK STREET ALLEY - DAY

Children play kickball between brick walls. A ball rockets through old MRS. ENGEL'S kitchen window. Twelve-year-old NICK steps forward as the other children scatter.

(SOUND: glass explodes, children gasp, distant traffic)

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Mark Conner kicked the ball right through Mrs. Engel's kitchen. Nick stepped up and took the blame.

NICK

I did it.

A week later, NICK quietly places sixty dollars in MRS. ENGEL'S hand.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

I had no idea where a twelve-year-old came up with sixty bucks. I was proud of him. None of us told Ma who really broke the window.

EXT. PIZZERIA - DAY

NARRATOR, in a work shirt with a bicycle beside him, reaches the pizzeria door as SIONNA trips on the pavement. She is pale and slender, with short brown hair, light-blue eyes, a black V-neck shirt, jeans, blue tennis shoes, a fish-symbol necklace, and a large backpack.

(SOUND: bicycle chain clicks, street traffic, the pizzeria doorbell)

NARRATOR

Are you okay?

SIONNA

Yes.

NARRATOR

I work here. I'm just going to work. What is your name?

SIONNA

Sionna.

NARRATOR

Sionna? That's different.

SIONNA

No thanks, I don't eat pizza, but if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes her number. As she walks away, she looks back over her shoulder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

It was like I knew her. Like she was looking straight through me.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Pain-pill bottles stand in a precise line across the kitchen counter. MA searches around them while a game show chatters from the living room.

(SOUND: television applause, a clock ticking)

NARRATOR

Ma, what's the matter?

MA

I can't find my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

NARRATOR

We don't go to church anymore. Your pills are right in front of you.

MA stares at him with empty, glassy eyes, then blinks back into the room.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

She was losing pieces of time. I knew I had to tell Nick.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

A Packers game flickers above the crowded bar. NICK, now a hulking factory worker in a brown suede jacket and heavy boots, slaps NARRATOR on the back.

(SOUND: bar chatter, pool balls crack, football crowd roars from the television)

NICK

I knew she would start going crazy cooped up in that place all day. Do something with her before she turns into some demented old fart and we have to pay for a nursing home.

NARRATOR

I just needed to get away from her for a while.

NICK

That's more like it.

Across the street, the library glows through the cold.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

I was angry at Nick. Then I saw the library, and went inside.

INT. LIBRARY - NIGHT

Old wood floors, hanging lamps, tall windows, and rows of books surround NARRATOR. MS. HART, the librarian, slides a temporary card across the desk.

(SOUND: radiator hiss, pages turning, wind against the windows)

NARRATOR

How do I take a book home from here?

MS. HART

Do you have a library card?

NARRATOR

No. How do I get one of those?

He fills out the form, then wanders into classic literature. His hand settles on Wuthering Heights.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Who needed school when everything was right here, in this building?

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

SIONNA, now with shoulder-length brown hair and the same pale face and watery blue eyes, sits at a small table with her backpack. NARRATOR faces her, holding Wuthering Heights.

(SOUND: espresso steaming, low student conversation, spoons against ceramic)

NARRATOR

What are you reading right now?

SIONNA

What are you reading?

NARRATOR

Wuthering Heights. It's the story of this guy and girl and how they grew apart but were really in love with each other.

SIONNA

Yes, I know. I read it.

She opens the book.

SIONNA

The environment of the book is like its people. They are wild and unpredictable. The love Heathcliff has for Catherine never changes, but in the end that kind of love destroys him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Something inside me was coming awake. She made me feel smarter just by being around her.

INT. MILWAUKEE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

NARRATOR lies awkwardly on a purple mat among twenty other students. SIONNA rests beside him, composed and graceful.

(SOUND: synchronized breathing, soft floor creaks)

SIONNA

You were really great at that.

NARRATOR

Are you kidding me?

SIONNA

Your practice is your practice.

NARRATOR

My practice is not the best practice.

SIONNA

That's why they call it “practice.”

They smile. NARRATOR closes his eyes, surprised by his own calm.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

NICK stands over MA's chair, fists clenching. NARRATOR enters, trying not to look afraid.

(SOUND: television murmurs, fists flex, the apartment door slams)

NICK

Ma missed her doctor's appointment yesterday, you ass. Where the hell were you?

NARRATOR

I totally forgot. I'm sorry. I was with someone.

NICK

With who?

NARRATOR

I met a girl.

NICK

A girl? That's worth all this?

NARRATOR

I said I was sorry!

NICK turns and leaves. MA watches the closed door.

INT. DOCTOR CARSON'S OFFICE - DAY

MA sits on an examination table, pale and confused. NARRATOR stands beside her, gripping a pill bottle.

(SOUND: fluorescent buzz, a distant hospital cart rattles past)

DR. CARSON

Your mother's blood pressure is dangerously high. I'm afraid of the risk of a stroke. Take her directly to the emergency room.

NARRATOR

I make sure she takes her pills religiously.

DR. CARSON

She will need blood pressure medicine intravenously until we can get her stabilized.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

They kept Ma in the hospital for three days. When she came home, Sionna helped me sort every pill into a blue container.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

The blue medication container sits open on the table. MA watches television. SIONNA, pale and light-eyed, sits beside NARRATOR as NICK enters in his boots and jacket.

(SOUND: door hinges groan, a television laugh track, pill compartments click shut)

NICK

Oh, holy shit. Aren't you Nurse Nancy?

NARRATOR

Sionna helped me figure it out.

NICK

This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

NARRATOR

Stop it, Nick.

NICK

Don't you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

NARRATOR

I'm not a bastard!

NICK

You're a giant pussy, and that makes you and me way different.

NICK slaps NARRATOR across the head.

(SOUND: sharp slap, ringing in NARRATOR'S left ear)

NARRATOR (V.O.)

In that split second, I knew I never wanted Sionna to meet that prick.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

The bar is packed. NICK, NARRATOR, and two of NICK'S friends stand near LINDSEY and her dark-haired boyfriend.

(SOUND: hard rock blares, glasses clink, pool balls scatter)

NICK

Who is this?

LINDSEY

Just an old friend.

NICK

I keep tabs on you. No one told me you were seeing anyone.

NARRATOR

Nick, leave it alone. Lindsey can see whoever she wants.

NICK

Keep out of it! You're going to be next if you don't get out of my face, asshole.

NARRATOR steps between NICK and the boyfriend. NICK'S fist draws back. JOE and BOUNCER HARRIS rush in.

(SOUND: sudden silence, a fist cuts the air, the bouncer is head-butted)

NARRATOR runs into the cold night.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

SIONNA, in a loose green top, sits on a green suede sofa. NARRATOR collapses beside her, breathless.

(SOUND: rain against the windows, ragged breathing slowly settles)

SIONNA

Your brother tried to hit you.

NARRATOR

He does care about me. I can't explain Nick completely.

SIONNA

No one does that, you know. I mean, no one who really cares about you.

She strokes the side of his face.

NARRATOR

The first time I met you, it was like I had finally come home.

SIONNA

Me too, honey. Me too.

NARRATOR rests his head in her lap.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

A calendar flips through months. Books, college papers, yoga clothes, and pill containers fill the apartment. SIONNA cooks while MA listens, and NARRATOR studies at the table.

(SOUND: pages turn, a pan sizzles, the television murmurs)

NARRATOR (V.O.)

My life became work, school, Ma, and Sionna. I was learning to think for myself.

NICK enters, now oddly attentive. He lays out MA's pills and kisses her cheek.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Then Nick began coming around more. He took Ma to church in his burnt-orange Pontiac Trans Am.

NICK

I'll take her to St. Patrick's for four o'clock.

MA smiles and reaches for his arm. NICK leads her out.

EXT. KIRK STREET ALLEY - NIGHT

The next morning, NARRATOR pedals toward the apartment. The windows are dark. His mother's purse and coat are missing.

(SOUND: bicycle tires hiss over wet pavement, a telephone answering machine beeps inside)

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

NARRATOR enters. The answering machine blinks: FIVE MISSED CALLS. He presses the button.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)

Mr. Olson, this is Officer Dupuis from the Milwaukee County Sheriff's Department. We need to talk to you regarding your family.

NARRATOR stares at the empty chair.

NARRATOR

Where the hell are they?

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

A stone police station surrounds NARRATOR with counters, phones, and waiting citizens. OFFICER DUPUIS, a large man with graying hair and a thin mustache, sits across from him.

(SOUND: ringing phones, typewriter keys, muffled voices)

OFFICER DUPUIS

There has been a serious accident involving your brother, Nicholas Olson, and your mother, Olivia Olson. They were hit broadside at Werner and Lowe. Your brother is in serious condition. Your mother passed away at the scene.

NARRATOR

Can I call someone? Can I have a ride to the hospital?

OFFICER DUPUIS

I know this is a huge shock. We will help you with whatever we can.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

NICK lies unconscious behind glass, bruised and wired to machines. His reddish hair sticks up around an oxygen tube. NARRATOR stands at the doorway, then turns away.

(SOUND: heart monitor beeps, oxygen hisses, gurney wheels rush past)

NARRATOR (V.O.)

His lung was punctured and his femur was broken. The Pontiac was totaled. Ma had to be pulled out with the jaws of life.

NARRATOR backs away from the room.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

I couldn't see him. Not then.

EXT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - DAY

A fresh grave rises beneath a pale sky. NARRATOR drops a handful of dirt onto the light maple-colored coffin. SIONNA stands beside him, one hand on his shoulder.

(SOUND: dirt strikes wood, wind moves through bare branches)

NARRATOR (V.O.)

I have fought the good fight. I have finished the race. I have kept the faith.

NARRATOR

Yes, you did, Ma.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Five days later, NICK lies propped in a hospital bed, sallow and bruised, a chest tube at his side and his right leg immobilized beneath the blanket.

(SOUND: television clicks off, heart monitor beeps steadily)

NICK

Hey, bro. I guess I fucked up, man. Can't believe Ma's gone.

NARRATOR

They took care of it. The mass was nice. She's at peace now.

NICK

I didn't mean to have three Jack and Cokes before I remembered I needed to take her to church. I can't believe this shit. The cops say it could be vehicular manslaughter.

NARRATOR

Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK

What do you mean? You think I meant to kill Ma?

NARRATOR

Think about it.

NARRATOR turns and leaves.

(SOUND: hospital door closes, the monitor continues without him)

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

SIONNA, pale and slender, her shoulder wrapped beneath a loose shirt, stands backed against the hallway wall. NICK looms over her, scarred face flushed, a switchblade open in his hand.

(SOUND: winter wind at the windows, the knife snaps open)

NICK

If it wasn't for you messing around with my brother, everything would be cool. He would be taking care of Ma. She would be alive right now.

SIONNA

Let's talk about this rationally.

NICK

You get some money, and you get the fuck away from my brother.

SIONNA

Nick, calm down. This really has nothing to do with me.

NICK grips her throat and presses the blade against her upper chest.

NICK

No more brother. No more contact. You got that?

The front door slams open. NARRATOR appears.

(SOUND: front door bangs against the wall)

NARRATOR

Nick, what the fuck are you doing?

NICK jerks toward him. The knife strikes SIONNA'S left shoulder and upper chest. She crumples. NICK drives an elbow into NARRATOR'S head and bolts.

(SOUND: impact, SIONNA'S shallow gasp, sirens rising outside)

NARRATOR drops beside her and presses both hands over the wound.

NARRATOR

My brother stabbed her. Her name is Sionna McAllister.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - DAY

SIONNA sits motionless in a chair, her arm supported by a foam pillow. NARRATOR kneels in front of her.

(SOUND: clock ticking, fabric shifts as she trembles)

NARRATOR

You are safe. You don't have to worry about anything hurting you. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA

I can't feel me. I can't feel myself anymore.

NARRATOR

I love you. You know that, right?

SIONNA

As long as there is any doubt, I can't go on with you. Maybe we just need a break.

She walks into the bedroom and closes the door.

(SOUND: bedroom door clicks shut, NARRATOR'S breath breaks into a sob)

INT. LOWELL'S RESTAURANT - DAY

Back in Seattle, the older NARRATOR sits at the same window with the same gray water beyond it. His hands rest beside the untouched cinnamon roll.

(SOUND: ferry horn, rain on glass, silverware against plates)

NARRATOR

She survived, but the wound stayed inside her. Eventually she left for her parents' farm. No amount of meditation or yoga can heal the deepest injuries.

He looks toward a framed photograph of a small pizza shop.

NARRATOR

Tony helped me. I built Tony's Boy's Pizza in Seattle. I learned to live alone. But I was still carrying Nick everywhere I went.

NARRATOR looks out at the city.

NARRATOR

I had to forgive him.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

A dozen people sit in candlelight. NARRATOR, older and stronger, closes his eyes on a mat.

(SOUND: low communal chanting, measured breathing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)

I had to start living again. I had to find forgiveness.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

NARRATOR sleeps on a couch. BOZO, an orange-and-white cat, curls against his stomach.

(SOUND: rain against the apartment windows, a cat purr)

In the dream, NARRATOR walks through brilliant green hills beside SAGE, a dark-skinned figure in a flowing white wrap.

SAGE

Stop judging things. This is not your place. Things are neither good nor bad, but thinking makes it so.

A discarded pizza box lies in the grass. NARRATOR looks down. When he looks again, it is gone.

NARRATOR jolts awake, grabs a notebook, and writes.

NARRATOR

I had to change. I had to start living again. I had to find forgiveness.

INT. GREEN BAY PRISON VISITATION ROOM - DAY

NARRATOR sits behind thick plexiglass. NICK enters on the other side, now thin and stooped in prison clothes, with graying hair, deep lines, and sad, watery eyes.

(SOUND: locks clank, fluorescent lights buzz, a phone receiver lifts)

NICK

Hi, bro. Glad you came.

NARRATOR

I'm here to tell you that even though you are my brother, I am no longer interested in hearing you control my life.

NICK lowers his eyes.

NARRATOR

You're an asshole. You're an asshole that killed my mother in a drunk-driving accident. You held me down my entire life. And you're an asshole for trying to kill the love of my life.

NICK begins to cry.

NARRATOR

But I forgive you. It sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

NICK

You're so right. I did all that shit. I am a huge asshole. You deserve to live out there. I deserve to live in here.

GUARD MILLER appears behind him.

GUARD MILLER

Time's almost up.

NARRATOR

I still believe in you. After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother.

NICK

What are you doing now?

NARRATOR

I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We'll be expanding.

NICK

President Olson. That's fucking amazing. I am so proud of you, man. I love you.

The guard leads NICK away.

(SOUND: phone receiver clicks down, prison doors lock one after another)

INT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - DAY

NARRATOR walks through the crowded terminal with a small bag. His boarding pass reads PETER OLSON.

(SOUND: boarding announcement, rolling luggage, jet engines beyond the glass)

NARRATOR looks down at the name.

NARRATOR

That's me. President Peter Olson.

INT. LOWELL'S RESTAURANT - DAY

NARRATOR sits by the window once more. He finally takes a bite of the cinnamon roll as sunlight breaks through the Seattle clouds.

(SOUND: ferry horn, coffee cup set gently on a saucer)

NARRATOR

This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END
